2 CONC _422341511/isabella-willett-born-standen  3  Added by confirming a Smart Match
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT James Willett married Isabella Standen, the daughter of wheelwright Lewis Standen and Maria Baker of Lamberhurst, Kent, on Thursday, December 13, 1838 at All Saints, Mountfield, near Battle, in Sussex. James was a blacksmith of ‘f
2 CONC ull age’, and Isabella was a ‘minor’. Their marriage was witnessed by Isabella’s brother Frederick and by Delia Buss. It is interesting to note that both the bride and groom as well as the witnesses had to sign their names with a 
2 CONC cross as they could not write. Their first two children were Maria and Semer (also referred to as ‘Seymour’, but the name ‘Semer’ is used in my branch of the Willett family, and is the name given on his death certificate, so it is
2 CONC  the name I will use in this report), and these children were still babies when the Willetts boarded the ship “Burhampooter” in Plymouth on 19th April, 1841, arriving in Australia 110 days later to begin their new lives. After the
2 CONC ir arrival in Sydney in August, 1841, the young Willett family spent the next few years in different locations throughout NSW, gradually heading south to Victoria around the time of the 1851 gold rush. James was a blacksmith, an o
2 CONC ccupation much sought after during that period, and so obtaining work would not have been a problem for him. Three additional sons were born during their time in NSW- James in 1843 in Campbelltown, Walter in 1846 and Stephen near 
2 CONC table Top Mountain, Mullengandra, close to Albury, in 1849. Walter died as an infant in 1848. On the death certificate of James Willett Jnr in 1875, his father states that James spent the first six years of his life in NSW, which 
2 CONC puts the family’s time of arrival in Victoria as c. 1849-50.   This completed the family of James Willett and Isabella Standen, although both went on to have children with another partner after they went their separate ways some t
2 CONC ime during the 1850s.   It seems as though both James and Isabella started their new lives on the Victorian goldfields- Isabella’s first child after separating from James was born in Ararat in 1858, to gold miner Henry Tanner. In 
2 CONC 1860 at Moliagul, near Newbridge, James’s new partner Mary Jane Bull bore him a son named Thomas. James Willett’s occupation always remained as ‘blacksmith’, even later when he was residing on a farm at Wangandary near Wangaratta.
2 CONC      Isabella formed a relationship with Henry Tanner, an Englishman from Gloucester. The 1858 birth certificate of their daughter stated that Henry was a thirty nine year old miner, and that his wife Isabella Tanner nee Standen w
2 CONC as twenty nine and from Hastings, Sussex. Isabella was the informant on the certificate, and signed her name with a cross as she was illiterate. She also fabricated some marriage details to give some legitimacy to her daughter’s b
2 CONC irth, stating that she and Henry had married in Sydney on May 10th, 1849.It was noted that the child, who was named Susan but known always as Emily, had two living siblings. I cannot find any record of these children being born be
2 CONC fore Susan, so most likely Isabella was referring to two of her younger children with James whom she may have taken with her when she separated from her husband. If so, these children would have been James and Stephen Willett, age
2 CONC d 15 and 9.  The goldfields around Avoca and Redbank attracted thousands upon thousands of miners during the 1850s, and Henry Tanner with Isabella Standen Willett joined the throng.  After their daughter was born in 1858, the coup
2 CONC le moved to     Redbank where son Henry Tanner was born in 1861.At this time, his father’s occupation was listed as ‘sawyer’. They were still living there when the little boy died in 1863, but son Lewis Tanner had his birth regist
2 CONC ered at Inglewood , another goldmining town, in 1865. The family was living at Avoca Forest, where Henry was still goldmining. At the time of registering her babies’ births, Isabella Standen gave varying ages for her ‘husband’- in
2 CONC  1858 she stated he was 39, yet in 1861 she gave his age as 37. A friend registered the birth of last child Lewis in 1865, and gave Henry’s age as 49, so the range of his year of birth as provided by birth certificates of his chil
2 CONC dren is 1816-1824.   The Tanner family eventually settled as farmers at Avoca Forest, and it was here that daughter Emily met and later married farmer Thomas Commings or Cummings (he was illiterate and signed his marriage certific
2 CONC ate with a cross, but his name was spelt ‘Commings’ on the certificate).    Henry Tanner Snr died in 1894, aged 79, and Isabella went to live with her daughter’s family. She died at the Cummings’ farm on July 16, 1907, at Berrimal
2 CONC , aged 86. Emily her daughter was the informant, and provided the following information:“ 16 July, 1907, Berrimal. Isabel Tanner, widow, 86 years. Cause of death: senile debility 3 months; heart failure 1 week. Parents: Lewis Stan
2 CONC den, carpenter, and Maria Baker. Info rmant: Emily Cummings, daughter, Berrimal Buried: Wedderburn Cemetery, 18 July, 1907. Born: Tunbridge Wells, England. 55 years in Victoria Marriage:  1st Marriage in Kent England aged 30 to __
2 CONC ___ Willett (actually looks like’Seymour’ instead of ‘James’). 2nd marriage Sydney NSW aged 36 to Henry Tanner. Issue: First marriage: Maria dead; Semmer 64; James dead; Walter dead; Stephen 60 years. Second marriage: Emily 49; He
2 CONC nry dead; Lewis 40.( NOTE: these ages were a little out- Semer was in fact 66, Stephen 58 and Lewis 42)
2 CONT
