3 CITY Lower Umgeni
3 STAE Natal
3 CTRY South Africa
1 OCCU Gardener
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Chick was born on 16th January , 1814 in Goudhurst in Kent...He died on 26th January , 1881 at Mont Rose , Lower Umgeni , Durban , SA. John married Philadelphia Collins ( B 1814 ) of Burwash in Sussex , UK on 
2 CONC 3rd April,1836 in Maidstone , Kent ,UK..Philadelphia died on 27 Nov, 1895 at Mont Rose , Lower Umgeni , Durban SA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before emmigrating to South Africa in 1850 John Chick and family had lived in Tunbridge...his address having been , Calverley Cottage , Tunbridge....They subsequently lived near Hampton Wick in Middlesex . According to recordings 
2 CONC made by his Son George , John had been trained as a Botanist and the Hampton Wick area west of London was noted for the production of fruit and flowers.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family emmigrated on the " Hebrides". It was recorded that there was a long delay in sailing and John Chick's funds were nearly exhausted by the time they reached Natal. He was thus unable to pay the survey fees on his allotme
2 CONC nt as a Hackett settler and only in June 1852 did he make any attempt to claim his land.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the first years in the Colony John was engaged in various occupations in different parts of Natal , eg a thatcher in Durban , a sawer in the Boston sawmills , and a gardener in Pietermaritzburg. They lived on Erf 22 Greyling S
2 CONC treet Pietermaritzburg during this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is presumed that it was John Chick who is recorded as a Ploughman on the farm of J.P. Symons at Hardings Dale near in the present Claridge area just outside Pietermaritzburg. John Chick and his Wife subsequently worked for a sh
2 CONC ort while for  Mr J.L. Feilden on  their farm "Feniscowles" on Durbans' Berea . The two families had major disagreements with Mrs Feilden being particularly derogatory toward Mrs Chick &  Mr Chick in turn apparently expressing him
2 CONC self as being " sick , tired and disgusted" with Feniscowles. Apparently both families were pleased to see the back of each other.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Chick remained in Durban and by 1855 was making his living as a sawer and general jobber. Around Set. 1857 he tendered to keep Durban's public drains - all 5300 yards of them - clean and in repair for the next twelve months. 
2 CONC His tender of £60.00 was viewed with consternation by the Town Council , but after a lengthy debate was accepted.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1858 the family settled at Lower Umgeni , Victoria County , and began cultivating arrowroot and a liitle later sugar. His sugar mill started crushing in 1864 and by 1870 it had been superseded by a modern steam mill...His Sons 
2 CONC William and Richard were by this time involved in the management of the estate. Like many planters Chick had to borrow finance at a heavy interest in order to install his sugar plant. This crippled him for some years but after a f
2 CONC ew years was able to pay off all his borrowings.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Johns' death in 1881 the estate was run under the name Chick Bros. William died in the following year and Richard carried on the enterprise , but under its old name, Rose Hill Sugar Estate. In 1897.  The homestead and surrou
2 CONC nding land was sold to the Coronation Brick and Tile Co......After Richard's death in 1905 his Widow together with sons Cyril and Horace continued with the estate until 1926. It was then sold to DurbanNorth Estates.
