1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Robert's early childhood was spent in wharf area of Sussex Street, Sydney.  At about the age of 6 or 7, (1852) he travelled with his family to the Victorian goldfields.  The rest of his childhood was spent on various goldfields in
2 CONC  the Bendigo district.       
2 CONT As a young man, he left Victoria for the gold rushes at Forbes.  In 1871 when gold was discovered at  Bushman's Lead (present-day Parkes), he joined the rush there.  A few months later, in April 1872, he married Maria McNevin, the
2 CONC  daughter of another miner.  Although no churches yet existed at Bushman's Lead, they were married according to the Rites of the Catholic Church.  Their marriage produced 14 children, but, sadly, as testimony to the difficulties o
2 CONC f life on the goldfields, 6 of their children died as infants.
2 CONT For the first 28 years of married life, Robert mined in the Forbes/ Parkes area.  On Christmas Eve, 1872, their first child, Anne Maria was born.  She became the first white child to be born at Parkes.  Robert at this time was wor
2 CONC king on the Bushman's Lead, one of the largest gold mines in the area.  There home was one of the hundreds of calico tents that comprised the town in these days.
2 CONT In 1874 the family had moved north west of Parkes to Goobang Creek.  Here, in May 1874, twin girls were born prematurely.  Unfortunately they lived only 32 hours.  Their graves are in the Old Cemetery, Goobang Creek.
2 CONT Between 1875 and 1898, the family lived at Wongajong, South Forbes.  Robert continued work as a miner.  Typhoid and dyptheria outbreaks were common on the goldfields.  In 1892, son James, aged 8, died from typhoid fever and, in 18
2 CONC 98, daughter, Lily, aged 10, died from dyptheria.
2 CONT About 1900, the family moved to the rapidly declining tin mining town of Tingha in northern NSW.   Since 1883 tin production had fallen markedly.  Except for a large number of Chinese, most miners had left Tingha.  However, the re
2 CONC ason for the family's move may be related to a group of gold miners from Victoria also came to Tingha in 1900 and started Australia's first tin dredge.  As they became successful, many other dredges started operation and Tingha wa
2 CONC s given new life.  
2 CONT Robert may have also operated a small farm or market garden at Tingha.  His daughter, Marion states on her marriage certificate that her father is a farmer at Tingha.  All miners were entitled to 1/4 acre of land at a cost of 2 sh
2 CONC illings 6 pence per year. There is a recollection that the  family may have grown and sold vegetables from the land they leased near the cemetery at Tingha.  
2 CONT On 14th May, 1909 Robert died at the Inverell Hospital.  Cause of death was the miner's disease, phthisis pulmonalis.  He was buried in the Church of England Cemetery at Tingha.
